{"graph_chars": "Supply is the amount of a good a producer is willing to produce and bring to market at each price. A rise in price almost always raises quantity supplied. A fall in price cuts it. That positive relationship, other things constant, is the law of supply. When gasoline prices rise, firms explore more, drill more, build pipelines, run refineries longer, keep stations open later. A supply schedule is the table. A supply curve is the graph, same axes as demand. At one dollar a gallon, five hundred million gallons supplied. At two dollars twenty, seven hundred twenty million. Nearly all supply curves slope up from left to right. Supply is the curve. Quantity supplied is a point. Do not mix them.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0653], [0.0653, 0.1305], [0.1305, 0.1958], [0.1958, 0.261], [0.261, 0.3263], [0.3263, 0.3915], [0.3915, 0.4568], [0.4568, 0.522], [0.522, 0.5873], [0.5873, 0.6525], [0.6525, 0.7178], [0.7178, 0.783], [0.783, 0.8483], [0.8483, 0.9135], [0.9135, 0.9788], [0.9788, 1.044], [1.044, 1.1093], [1.1093, 1.1745], [1.1745, 1.2398], [1.2398, 1.305], [1.305, 1.3703], [1.3703, 1.4355], [1.4355, 1.5008], [1.5008, 1.566], [1.566, 1.6313], [1.6313, 1.6965], [1.6965, 1.7618], [1.7618, 1.827], [1.827, 1.8923], [1.8923, 1.9575], [1.9575, 2.0228], [2.0228, 2.088], [2.088, 2.1533], [2.1533, 2.2185], [2.2185, 2.2838], [2.2838, 2.349], [2.349, 2.4143], [2.4143, 2.4795], [2.4795, 2.5448], [2.5448, 2.61], [2.61, 2.6753], [2.6753, 2.7405], [2.7405, 2.8058], [2.8058, 2.871], [2.871, 2.9363], [2.9363, 3.0015], [3.0015, 3.0668], [3.0668, 3.1321], [3.1321, 3.1973], [3.1973, 3.2626], [3.2626, 3.3278], [3.3278, 3.3931], [3.3931, 3.4583], [3.4583, 3.5236], [3.5236, 3.5888], [3.5888, 3.6541], [3.6541, 3.7193], [3.7193, 3.7846], [3.7846, 3.8498], [3.8498, 3.9151], [3.9151, 3.9803], [3.9803, 4.0456], [4.0456, 4.1108], [4.1108, 4.1761], [4.1761, 4.2413], [4.2413, 4.3066], [4.3066, 4.3718], [4.3718, 4.4371], [4.4371, 4.5023], [4.5023, 4.5676], [4.5676, 4.6328], [4.6328, 4.6981]